\section{An Interface Constrains What Can Be Sent}

Calling a method through an interface is a static promise about the message, not a static choice of the method body. Martin Abadi and Luca Cardelli's \emph{A Theory of Objects} distinguishes object operations from questions of subclassing and subtyping. A useful compiler analogue is to prove that a receiver supports a call with a certain signature separately from selecting its concrete implementation. A method can return a base interface and still dynamically produce a more specific object. Inheritance can implement this interface, but sharing source code is not in itself proof of substitutability.

Joseph C.\ Vanderwaart's \emph{Typed Intermediate Representations for Compiling Object-Oriented Languages} studies the type information needed to encode classes and objects in a typed intermediate language. A schematic encoding for an interface with one method $\mathsf{area}:\mathsf{Unit}\to\mathsf{Float}$ is
\[
 \exists X.\ \{
   \mathsf{self}:X,\quad
   \mathsf{area}:X\to\mathsf{Unit}\to\mathsf{Float}
 \}.
\]
The client opens the package and obtains an abstract $X$. It can pass the packaged receiver to the packaged method because both mention that same $X$. It cannot pass a receiver extracted from a different package, even if both packages implement the same interface. A record of visible operations thus exposes how to use a receiver without exposing its layout. Full object encodings may need extra machinery for mutation, recursive self types, or late binding inside method bodies.

\section{Worked Dispatch: Check the Receiver Used by the Call}

Suppose $\mathsf{Shape}$ exposes $\mathsf{area}$ and $\mathsf{Circle}$ implements it with a stored radius. A baseline call on $v:\mathsf{Shape}$ selects the appropriate interface slot and passes $v$ as receiver. A specialized path in a nominal runtime might be:
\begin{verbatim}
v = load(shapeSlot)
if exact_class(v) == Circle:
    r = Circle_area(v, ())
else:
    r = dispatch_area(v, ())
\end{verbatim}
Assume a nonnull receiver and a stable method table, so the exact-class test proves that the receiver has the circle layout and the slot resolves to \texttt{Circle\_area}. The result join has type $\mathsf{Float}$ on both edges. Importantly, both the guard and call use $v$. Reloading \texttt{shapeSlot} after a call could observe another object, and a subtype test that also admits subclasses is inadequate if they may override $\mathsf{area}$. Conversely, an exact-class guard does not validate mutable method bindings in a runtime that allows class redefinition. Such a runtime must check or track a version dependency as well.

The invariant is that a direct target must equal the lookup result for the exact receiver and method-table state at that point, with any receiver adjustment required by the calling convention. If those facts cannot be established, keep dynamic dispatch. This is independent of the type checker proving that the method result can be used as a float. A high-confidence receiver profile helps choose a fast path; it does not establish that this invariant holds without a guard.

\section{Exceptions to the Simple Slot Story}

Suppose the same interface also exposes $\mathsf{intersects}:\mathsf{Shape}\to\mathsf{Bool}$. An override cannot narrow its accepted argument to $\mathsf{Circle}$ while remaining usable at the original signature: a client selecting it through $\mathsf{Shape}$ may pass a rectangle. Self-dependent binary methods need an explicit relationship between receiver and argument, or a checked dynamic operation. Similarly, devirtualizing a call made by an inherited method on its own receiver must respect possible subclass overrides. The class that supplied a body need not be the receiver's class.

Urs H\"olzle, Craig Chambers, and David Ungar's \emph{Optimizing Dynamically-Typed Object-Oriented Languages with Polymorphic Inline Caches} uses several receiver-specific lookup results per call site and uses the observed receivers to inform adaptive compilation. In a compiler that uses this technique, a cache hit can refine the receiver on its guarded edge, a miss can perform normal lookup, and too many cases can justify reverting to generic dispatch. Test an overriding subclass, a site alternating receiver classes, and table invalidation if updates are allowed. Markdown Dossier Chapter~03 covers speculative IR; the distinction here is between a static interface, a conditional type refinement, and an observation used only to predict which path will be hot.
